+    <section class="journal">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From origin to cup</p>
+      <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <p>A closer look at the people, places, and decisions behind our beans.</p>
+
+      <div class="journal-copy" id="journal-text">
+        <p>A good cup of coffee begins long before we switch on the roaster. Our sourcing process starts with a conversation: who grew this coffee, what made this harvest distinctive, and what does the producer need to keep growing well next season? We look for partners who can connect a lot to its farm, cooperative, or washing station. Knowing that journey helps us understand the coffee as an agricultural crop shaped by people and place, rather than simply a flavor on a tasting sheet.</p>
+
+        <p>We work with producers, cooperatives, and importers who can share the story behind a harvest and explain how the coffee was purchased. We ask about pricing, harvest conditions, and the challenges facing the people doing the work. A great sample is the start of a relationship, not the end of our research. Our aim is to build repeat partnerships that give growers a reason to invest in quality and give us a deeper understanding of their work from one season to the next.</p>
+
+        <p>Before choosing a lot, we taste samples side by side in a process called cupping. We assess sweetness, acidity, body, balance, and the finish, returning to each cup as it cools. We also consider how the beans were processed. Washed coffees can offer a clear, bright expression of their origin, while naturally processed coffees can bring a fuller fruit character. Neither method is automatically better; we look for careful handling and a flavor profile that feels complete, whether it is destined for espresso or a slow weekend pour-over.</p>
+
+        <p>Environmental care is part of that conversation, too. We ask our sourcing partners about shade trees, soil health, water use, and the handling of processing waste. Growing conditions vary widely, so we favor specific information over sweeping promises. We want to understand what is working on a particular farm and where there is room to improve. Traceability gives us a way to keep asking better questions and to make more thoughtful purchasing decisions as we learn.</p>
+
+        <p>Once a coffee arrives, we compare it with the original sample and develop a roast profile in small batches. We adjust time and temperature to bring out the character we tasted at origin, then cup the results before putting the coffee on the menu. Our selection changes with harvest seasons because freshness and quality matter more than keeping every origin available year-round. From the first sample to the final roast, the goal stays the same: honor the work behind each bean and share a cup that makes its journey worth knowing.</p>
+      </div>
